Fillets: at a corner whose set-back walked over a vertex (1801), the
plate's border from the point on the edge walked onto, on the face that
goes on along that edge, leaves the point at no set angle, so that the
plate's face can be triangulated (mitcad#2018).

The bent guide of 1801 with its bend radius at 24.5 or 25: the rounding of
the inner sloped edge of the rib 1 degree from each end ends in a corner
whose set-back walks over the inner rim's end vertex onto the pad's rim
(0.11 along it), the plate's border on the tube's side crossing the bend's
end edge just past the vertex. The border on the flat from the point on
the pad's rim was a batten held at both ends to the angle of the sharp
edge's direction there: it left the point back along the rim, over the
crossing, and the plate there was a sliver 0.01 wide between it and the
border along the rim, the crossing's vertex in it. The plate's surface is
approximated within 0.04 (its edges' tolerances 0.05, its vertices' 0.08):
BRepMesh's triangulation of that face came out empty ("a face could not be
triangulated" on an STL export at a deviation of 0.01 and an angle of 10
degrees, at 0.05 and 5 degrees; and at other settings from 20 to 28).

- PerformMoreThreeCorner, in its connecting curves: where the corner walked
  over a vertex, the batten from the point on the edge walked onto towards
  the next sharp edge (not towards the tangent edge) has no angle at that
  point (CalculBatten's constraint there off, as for a free border); its
  other end keeps its angle. The border leaves the point towards its other
  end, and the sliver and the vertex in it are gone.
- Elsewhere nothing changes. No catch is added.

Test: cli.fcstd_bend_reported, the reported bent guide with its bend
radius set to 24.5 and 25 exported as STL at deviations 0.0005, 0.002, 0.01
and 0.05 mm with an angle of 10 degrees.

